# Two Mandatory Button Rules

Classify every enabled control before styling it. A control belongs to exactly one family: page-changing navigation or a local/in-page action. Disabled controls are the only interaction-state exception.

## Rule 1 — Page-changing navigation

Use this rule only for a real anchor whose normal action replaces the current document with another same-origin pathname. Mark it with `data-page-navigation="button"` and retain its meaningful `href`.

- Rest at 12px corners. On hover, round in place over 200ms without changing position, width, height, label, or icon scale.
- On activation, run the OCS center-out theme-ink wave for 350ms at 14% opacity with a 71% final circular clip.
- Keep the rounded silhouette through the first half, return toward 12px corners during the second half, then hold the filled squarer state until navigation replaces the document.
- Wait for the actual wave animation to finish before replaying the original link once. Use a finite 350ms fallback only when animation completion is unavailable.
- Never delay external links, downloads, modified/new-tab clicks, or in-page hash navigation. Preserve Back/page-restoration cleanup and reduced-motion immediate navigation.
- Use a real anchor, visible keyboard focus, and the original navigation semantics. Without JavaScript, the link must still work.

## Rule 2 — Local and in-page controls

Use this rule for every native button, role-based button, tab, disclosure, task/calendar action, settings action, menu, in-page route, hash link, and dialog or panel launcher.

- The action is immediate. Never wait for the page-navigation wave or add an artificial navigation delay.
- Hover gives the existing DuckPlan tint/elevation change. Press gives immediate held compression/brightness feedback. Release gives the existing brief expansion/settle response.
- If the control opens a dialog, sheet, drawer, menu, or panel, expand that UI immediately from the launcher using the established popup/layout motion. Simple toggles and commands keep the tint/press/release response without inventing a popup.
- Repeated calendar cells, time slots, task rows, and event blocks stay lightweight without individual backdrop filters; use their existing tint/outline press states.
- The sidebar continues to reshape the workspace in the same layout plane. It must not use a page-navigation delay or wave.
- Popup identity continues its launcher tint/material and action wording. Preserve focus, Escape/backdrop dismissal, interruption cleanup, stable bounds, and reduced-motion behavior.

## Shared requirements

- Every enabled control in either family must have visible hover, activation, keyboard-focus, and reduced-motion feedback. Disabled controls show none of the interaction effects and look unavailable.
- Glass remains the planner material with crisp native labels and icons. Fur remains opt-in inside its dedicated labs; do not mount WebGL fur in the planner.
- Preserve semantic labels and accessible names. Icon-only controls require explicit `aria-label` text.
- Interaction effects must not shift surrounding layout or reduce readability.
- Layout and popup motion use the shared tokens in `landing/motion-tokens.js`; page-navigation shape and wave feedback use the handbook's 200ms and 350ms timings.
- Verify both families whenever buttons change: page-link hover, wave, held shape, single navigation, modified clicks, Back and reduced motion; local hover, held press, release/settle, immediate action, popup expansion where applicable, keyboard, disabled and reduced motion.
